Kind: ablation
Issue: #748
Over: 748-widen-knock-after-cpu-up.patch
Takes out: ast_check()'s return for a processor with no thread yet -- over the widen, each knocked processor goes down the RUNNING arm to the null activation and GCC's ud2 stops the machine, as processor 3's did at entry 41
Run: --entry 0 600 -smp 4
Expect: trap invalid opcode \(vector

diff --git a/uros/src/mach_kernel/kern/ast.c b/uros/src/mach_kernel/kern/ast.c
index 96f4f544..155689bf 100644
--- a/uros/src/mach_kernel/kern/ast.c
+++ b/uros/src/mach_kernel/kern/ast.c
@@ -306,25 +306,8 @@ ast_check(void)
 	register int		mycpu;
 	register processor_t	myprocessor;
 	register thread_t	thread = current_thread();
-	spl_t			s;
+	spl_t			s = splsched();
 
-	/*
-	 * 🔴 NO THREAD YET, NOTHING TO CHECK (#748).
-	 *
-	 * cpu_up() makes a processor PROCESSOR_RUNNING before
-	 * cpu_launch_first_thread() gives it a thread, so for a few lines a
-	 * running processor has none.  An AST interrupt there -- on x86-64 the
-	 * scheduler gate's knock, which cpu_up()'s splx() lets through -- came
-	 * down the RUNNING arm below to current_act(), dereferenced the null
-	 * activation and stopped the machine.  With no thread there is no
-	 * activation whose ASTs to propagate and no thread to switch away from:
-	 * the first one is about to be loaded, and the check is answered by
-	 * doing nothing.
-	 */
-	if (thread == THREAD_NULL)
-		return;
-
-	s = splsched();
 	mp_disable_preemption();
 	mycpu = cpu_number();
 
